   "h2": "Five adverbs to learn",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "These five are on the Chapter 3 list as they are. Most say when or how long."
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Latin",
       "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "<em>deinde</em>",
        "then, next"
       ],
       [
        "<em>diu</em>",
        "for a long time"
       ],
       [
        "<em>statim</em>",
        "at once, immediately"
       ],
       [
        "<em>subito</em>",
        "suddenly"
       ],
       [
        "<em>tandem</em>",
        "at last, finally"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "nuntius <u class=\"c\">statim</u> <u class=\"v\">currit</u>.",
      "en": "The messenger runs <b>at once</b>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servi <u class=\"c\">diu</u> <u class=\"v\">laborabant</u>.",
      "en": "The slaves were working <b>for a long time</b>.",
      "note": "<em>diu</em> answers \"how long?\", like the accusative of time."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating them",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "An adverb usually comes just before the verb it describes.",
      "In English it can go before or after the verb: \"he angrily shouts\", \"he shouts angrily\".",
      "<em>subito</em>, <em>tandem</em> and <em>deinde</em> often sound best at the start of the English: \"At last the slaves arrive.\""
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">subito</u> nautae <u class=\"v\">discedunt</u>.",
      "en": "<b>Suddenly</b> the sailors leave."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "<em>laete</em> is an adverb, \"happily\". <em>laeta</em> is an adjective, \"happy\", and needs a noun to agree with.",
      "An adverb never changes: <em>irate</em> is the same with <em>servus</em> or <em>servi</em>.",
      "<em>diu</em> is \"for a long time\": how long something went on.",
      "<em>deinde</em> is \"then\" in the sense of \"next\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
